Backport: Support presenting to a native Wayland surface on Qt

Upstream commit:
    https://github.com/pygfx/rendercanvas/commit/0c4de5fcd37bf0e359adcdec179782a267612dd0
Upstream PR:
    https://github.com/pygfx/rendercanvas/pull/233

On Wayland, rendercanvas put Qt on XWayland, and the code to present to a
native Wayland surface was disabled with an `if False`. That code could not
work: it paired the wl_surface from winId() with a display obtained from
get_alt_wayland_display(), i.e. a fresh wl_display_connect(). A surface is a
proxy owned by the connection that created it, so libwayland aborts with
"Proxy and queue point to different wl_displays" as soon as a buffer is
attached.

This asks Qt for the wl_display it is itself connected to, decides the
platform from the qpa plugin Qt actually uses (under a Wayland session Qt may
still be on XWayland, where winId() is an X11 window), and only forces
XWayland when the Qt lib cannot provide its display. It also releases the wgpu
surface on close instead of leaving it to the garbage collector, which on
Wayland is a use-after-free at interpreter shutdown.

Backport notes: the upstream commit also touches docs/backends.rst and
tests/test_backend_qt.py, neither of which ships in the PyPI sdist, so those
hunks are dropped. The remaining hunks were re-generated against the 2.7.2
sdist (qt.py has an extra `import weakref` and a small line offset relative to
the upstream parent commit); the content is otherwise unchanged.

